"MALLM: Multi-Agent Large Language Models Framework (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.11656v1)\nciteturn3188view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3184view2\",\"lineno\":170}); Total lines: 539\nL123: ##### Persona Generator.\nL124: \nL125: The persona specifies agent behavior by their system prompt, e.g., expertise or personality (cite62†Xu et al., 2023 ; cite61†Wang et al., 2023 ). Personas are created iteratively to be complementary and unique. We include three persona types: None, Expert, and IPIP.\nL126: None: Disables the persona generation. It assigns each agent a generic name (“Participant N”) for baseline experiments. Expert: Creates domain-specific personas aligned with the task description (cite62†Xu et al., 2023 ). Examples are an “Educator” for machine learning explanations, a “Software Developer” for app development, or a “Chef” for cooking tasks. IPIP: Based on the Big Five personality traits, using the open-source IPIP-NEO classification (cite77†Costa and McCrae, 1992 ; cite78†Goldberg et al., 1999 ).\nL127: They cover Extraversion, Agreeableness, Conscientiousness, Neuroticism, and Openness. The items originate from the International Personality Item Pool (IPIP), frequently used in Psychology (cite79†Maples et al., 2014 ). This enables the detailed modeling of psychological diversity (cite80†Serapio-García et al., 2023 ; cite81†Sorokovikova et al., 2024 ).\nL128: ##### Response Generator.\nL129: \nL130: The response generator produces agent responses in a specified format or style, influencing how agents interact (e.g., neutral or critical) (cite82†Mizrahi et al., 2024 ). They vary in how the agents are prompted to generate a response in the debate. MALLM includes the Simple, Reasoning, and Critical response generators. We include their prompts in cite33†Section G.3 .\nL131: Simple: Produces free-text responses in a neutral tone, explicitly indicating agreement or disagreement. Reasoning: Responds step-by-step, including analysis, alternatives, and conclusions. Agents share their reasoning but not solutions, encouraging independent idea generation. Critical: Tasks the agent to identify weaknesses, question assumptions, and suggest alternative approaches.\nL132: ### 3.2 Discussion Paradigm\nL133: \nL134: The discussion paradigm defines the structure of agent interaction. It specifies turn-taking and information access rules for the MAD. We implement four paradigms from cite63†Yin et al. (2023) : Memory, Report, Relay, and Debate. Each paradigm differs in information flow and visibility, as illustrated in cite83†Figure 2 .\nL135: Memory: All agents have full visibility into each other’s messages across turns. Relay: Information is passed sequentially between agents in a chain, with only the last message visible to the next. Report: Agents independently solve the tasks and report back to a central agent. Debate: Agents argue in pairs, taking turns to debate intermediate conclusions before a central agent is consulted.\nL136: \nL137: Figure 2: Overview of four discussion paradigms.\nL138: ### 3.3 Decision Protocol\nL139: \nL140: Each agent in a multi-agent system generates solution drafts. Decision protocols systematically determine when discussions end and combine agent-generated solutions into a final decision.\nL141: \nL142: Figure 3: Overview of three main decision protocols.\nL143: \nL144: The MALLM framework implements three decision protocol families: Consensus, Voting, and Judge, as illustrated in cite84†Figure 3 .\nL145: Consensus: Consensus decision protocols decide on an answer by having the agents converge on one solution. The solution is selected when a required level of agreement among agents is reached (cite63†Yin et al., 2023 ). MALLM includes three agreement levels: Majority Consensus (over 50%), Supermajority Consensus (over 66%), and Unanimity Consensus (100%). Voting: Uses a fixed number of discussion rounds (default three, following findings by cite54†Du et al. (2023) ) before agents vote.\nL146: In the event of a tie, we run an additional round of debate and voting. Variants are inspired by cite73†Yang et al. (2024) and include Simple Voting (each agent votes once for their preferred solution), Approval Voting (multiple acceptable solutions per agent), Ranked Voting (agents rank solutions, best cumulative rank selected), and Cumulative Voting (agents allocate up to 25 points across solutions, highest total points selected).\nL147: Judge: Relies on one agent reviewing all solutions, choosing either a preferred one or synthesizing a new solution. The effectiveness of the Judge protocol depends on the accuracy of one agent’s reasoning (cite85†Zheng et al., 2023 ).\nL148: ### 3.4 Evaluation\nL149: \nL150: The MALLM framework includes a pipeline for evaluating MAD configurations, producing statistics and charts for a dataset and its metrics.\nL151: Datasets. The pipeline provides integrated loaders for reasoning tasks (e.g., WinoGrande (cite86†Sakaguchi et al., 2019 ), StrategyQA (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 )) and knowledge tasks (e.g., GPQA (cite88†Rein et al., 2023 ), MMLU-Pro (cite89†Wang et al., 2024c )) as well as core tasks of text generation (cite90†Becker et al., 2024 ), such as paraphrasing (cite91†Kovatchev et al., 2018 ) or summarization (cite92†Narayan et al., 2018 ). It further supports any textual Huggingface dataset for problem-solving.\nL152: Researchers can also add their own dataset via subclassing. All datasets are converted into a unified format for processing.\nL153: Metrics. We include question-answering and free-text evaluations. For question-answering, we compute accuracy by comparing selected response letters against reference solutions via regex. For free-text tasks, we include BERTScore (cite93†Zhang et al., 2020 ) and textual overlap measures (BLEU (cite94†Papineni et al., 2002 ), ROUGE-1/2/3/L (cite95†Lin, 2004 ), and METEOR (cite96†Banerjee and Lavie, 2005 )).\nL154: Statistical variance. We find that several studies on MAD do not account for statistical variance (cite66†Wu et al., 2023 ; cite97†Talebirad and Nadiri, 2023 ), yet it can have marked impacts on MAD (cite74†Smit et al., 2024 ). MALLM enables repeated experiments and calculates standard deviations between them, thereby quantifying statistical robustness.\nL155: Automatic charts. MALLM can visualize the evaluation results of MAD configurations. For this, researchers can pass a single file or a directory with multiple evaluation results directly to an automatic chart generator. Examples of the generated charts by the evaluation pipeline can be seen in cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite100†9 and cite101†8 of cite29†Appendix F .\nL156: ## 4 Application\nL157: We explain the workflow for using MALLM to evaluate a specific MAD configuration. First, we can load any text-based Huggingface dataset for problem-solving via our dataset loader (e.g., MMLU-Pro (cite89†Wang et al., 2024c )). Second, MALLM comes with a configuration file (cf.\nL158: cite42†Appendix H ) that the user can change to adjust parameters and the components for the desired experiment (e.g., using Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct as a model and changing the configuration to four debating agents, Relay discussion paradigm, and Unanimity Voting decision protocol). Third, we can run the experiment specified by the configuration file. MALLM keeps extensive logs of all debates.\nL159: Lastly, we can directly feed the logs into our evaluation pipeline, computing metrics (e.g., accuracy) and leveraging automatic chart generation (cf. cite98†Figures 10 , cite99†7 , cite101†8 and cite100†9 in cite29†Appendix F ).\nL160: Each component (agent, discussion, decision) comes with abstract base classes to describe the pipeline of MAD. If necessary, a custom component could be provided by inheriting features from a component’s abstract base class. For example, Sketch-of-Thought (cite102†Aytes et al., 2025 ) just got published, a variant of Chain-of-Thought (cite103†Wei et al., 2023 ) that restructures how models express intermediate steps of their reasoning.\nL161: To integrate with MALLM, we would create a custom class SoTResponseGenerator inheriting from the abstract base class ResponseGenerator. Then, MAD can be defined by a configuration file as usual. cite104†Figure 5 in cite21†Appendix A provides an overview of the workflow for conducting and evaluating MAD with MALLM.\nL162: ### 4.1 Use Cases\nL163: \nL164: The following examples illustrate possible research directions that can be realized using MALLM.\nL165: \nL166: Agents on paradigms. The number of agents for the MAD is modifiable. Researchers can test the impact of this on the various discussion paradigms. For example, a study could compare two, three, four, or five agents on the memory paradigm with the relay paradigm. As these paradigms differ in their information flow, it would be interesting to see how this impacts task performance.\nL167: Testing new task. JailbreakBench (cite105†Chao et al., 2024 ) measures the safety of LLMs against jailbreaks. One avenue could be the comparison of multi-agent safety with a single-agent setup. For this, we can use MALLM, which is plug-and-play with a templated configuration set.\nL168: Finetuned agents. MALLM works with any proprietary and open LLM, which means that we can also provide our own model for the agents. A promising direction is to fine-tune an agent to enhance its argumentation skills, thereby improving task performance on reasoning tasks such as StrategyQA (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 ).\nL169: Moderated paradigm. A dynamic moderator can be implemented by subclassing the abstract base class DiscussionParadigm. We can define logic for an LLM-based moderator agent to adjust speaking order based on previous agent contributions. This enables an investigation into the effects of adaptive moderation.\nL170: ### 4.2 Example Experiments with MALLM\nL171: \nL172: MALLM can be applied to various use cases. To demonstrate the opportunities for experimental setups, we provide some example investigations. Supplementary information, such as model parameters, can be found in cite25†Appendix E .\nL173: Agents. cite65†Kaesberg et al. (2025) use MALLM to experiment with response generators on the StrategyQA dataset (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 ). cite106†Table 1 presents the average accuracy across all voting-based decision protocols, using the Memory discussion paradigm and Expert personas with the Simple, Critical, and Reasoning response generators. The Critical response generator slightly improves performance by encouraging agents to critically evaluate responses from others, resulting in a 2.8% point increase.\nL174: The Reasoning response generator decreases performance by 6.4% points, likely because it imposes a strict response structure. Strictly structured responses can degrade the task performance of MAD, a characteristic that was previously noted in single-agent setups (cite107†Tam et al., 2024 ). To summarize, invoking strict response patterns from agents can harm task performance, while prompting agents to think critically can boost it.\nL175: Simple  | Critical  | Reasoning\nL176: 58.6$\\pm$1.6  | 61.4$\\pm$3.3  | 52.2$\\pm$2.8\nL177: Table 1: Comparison of accuracy averaged over all voting-based decision protocols using the simple, critical, and reasoning response generators on the StrategyQA dataset. $\\pm$ shows standard deviation over three runs.\nL178: Discussion Paradigms. We experiment with discussion paradigms on the StrategyQA dataset (cite87†Geva et al., 2021 ), using Expert personas, the Simple response generator, and Majority Consensus. MAD runs until the agent agrees to a solution or until a maximum of seven turns is reached. We find that, using Majority Consensus, most debates reach an agreement and end within the first three turns. Thus, seven turns provide a reasonable headroom for this experiment. Experimental results on MAD reveal two findings.\nL179: First, cite108†Table 2 compares the discussion paradigms of MAD against a single LLM baseline with cite109†CoT (cite103†Wei et al., 2023 ). All paradigms outperform a single LLM with cite109†CoT prompting on StrategyQA, improving accuracy by up to 4.0%. Second, we investigate the impact of information transparency on convergence speed for MAD. We find that the very transparent paradigm Memory enables faster consensus (avg. 1.75 turns), while limited visibility between agents in Relay slows it down (avg.\nL180: 2.61 turns). Thus, information transparency can lead to quicker convergence in MAD without sacrificing task performance. In summary, MAD can outperform Chain-of-Thought on reasoning tasks, such as StrategyQA, while the information transparency of the discussion paradigm noticeably impacts the convergence speed of MAD.\nL181: CoT  | Memory  | Relay  | Report  | Debate\nL182: 56.9$\\pm$1.8  | 60.8$\\pm$2.6  | 62.9$\\pm$1.6  | 60.9$\\pm$3.1  | 61.9$\\pm$1.1\nL183: Table 2: Accuracy of MAD on StrategyQA with different discussion paradigms. Best and worst are bolded. $\\pm$ shows standard deviation over five runs.\nL184: Decision Protocols. Investigations by cite65†Kaesberg et al. (2025) compare the average of all Voting and Consensus decision protocols across knowledge and reasoning tasks, summarized in cite110†Table 3 . They use the Simple response generator and the Memory discussion paradigm. Consensus consistently outperforms Voting on knowledge tasks (e.g., MMLU-Pro, GPQA), achieving approximately 2.8% higher accuracy due to repeated verification steps.\nL185: Voting protocols significantly improve accuracy by about 13.2% on reasoning-intensive tasks (e.g., SQuAD 2.0, StrategyQA, MuSR), benefiting from diverse reasoning paths. To summarize, the selection of the decision protocol depends on the specific task. When chosen correctly, it can notably improve task performance.\nL186: Dataset  | Voting  | Consensus\nL187: Knowledge-Based  |  |\nL188:    MMLU  | 51.7 $\\pm$ 2.4  | 54.0 $\\pm$ 2.7\nL189:    MMLU-Pro  | 31.1 $\\pm$ 3.5  | 36.0 $\\pm$ 1.8\nL190:    GPQA  | 29.7 $\\pm$ 2.5  | 31.0 $\\pm$ 2.4\nL191: Reasoning-Based  |  |\nL192:    SQuAD 2.0  | 56.7 $\\pm$ 1.6  | 43.6 $\\pm$ 1.5\nL193:    StrategyQA  | 58.6 $\\pm$ 2.0  | 58.4 $\\pm$ 1.6\nL194:    MuSR  | 54.8 $\\pm$ 1.9  | 28.4 $\\pm$ 2.6\nL195: Table 3: Mean performance for voting and consensus decision protocols on knowledge and reasoning tasks. $\\pm$ shows standard deviation over three runs.\nL196: Creating Demo Examples. For the demonstration of MALLM, we created a set of 144 different example configurations using MALLM’s batch feature, called DEBATE (D iverse E xchanges B etween A utonomous T alking E ntities)^{3}^{3} 3 cite111†huggingface.co/datasets/Multi-Agent-LLMs/DEBATE†huggingface.co .\nL197: Potential avenues for this data could be to (1) study how agents debate as a proxy to humans; (2) study the structural reasons why MAD can fail in some scenarios, as identified by (cite112†Becker et al., 2025 ); (3) explore how prompting agents to assess prior messages critically affects the speed of consensus-building. More details are in cite24†Appendix D .\nL198: ## 5 Epilogue\nL199: We proposed MALLM, a framework for MAD to conduct and evaluate conversational problem-solving. MALLM enables systematic studies of core MAD components and their interplay: agent personas, response generators, discussion paradigms, and decision protocols. Our framework works with most proprietary and open models and can load any text-based Huggingface dataset for problem-solving.\nL200: An included evaluation pipeline offers pre-implemented metrics (e.g., Accuracy, BLEU, BERTScore) and automatic chart generation, while accounting for the statistical variance of MAD. We presented the capabilities of MALLM on our public demo website. Regardless of whether researchers conduct a systematic analysis of MAD configurations, undertake a new task, or employ a new agent, MALLM provides a straightforward environment for conducting plug-and-play investigations in the field of MAD.\nL201: ## Limitations\nL202: We provide the MALLM framework with pre-implemented variants of each component (personas, response generators, discussion paradigm, decision protocol). While our goal was to include diverse variants backed by literature (e.g., voting (cite73†Yang et al., 2024 ), consensus (cite63†Yin et al., 2023 ), and judge (cite85†Zheng et al., 2023 ) for decision protocols), we could not account for all possible patterns of agent orchestration.\nL203: There is the possibility that niche use cases with MAD and future developments are not captured by our selection. We publish our framework as open-source, allowing researchers to develop custom components via subclassing and apply MALLM to their specific use cases.\nL204: ## 6 Acknowledgements\nL205: \nL206: This work was supported by the Landeskriminalamt NRW, the Lower Saxony Ministry of Science and Culture and the VW Foundation.\nL207: ## References\nL208:   * Aytes et al. (2025) Simon A. Aytes, Jinheon Baek, and Sung Ju Hwang. 2025. cite113†Sketch-of-thought: Efficient llm reasoning with adaptive cognitive-inspired sketching . Preprint, arXiv:2503.05179.\nL209:   * Banerjee and Lavie (2005) Satanjeev Banerjee and Alon Lavie. 2005. cite114†METEOR: An automatic metric for MT evaluation with improved correlation with human judgments†aclanthology.org . In Proceedings of the ACL Workshop on Intrinsic and Extrinsic Evaluation Measures for Machine Translation and/or Summarization, pages 65–72, Ann Arbor, Michigan. Association for Computational Linguistics.\nL210:   * Becker et al. (2025) Jonas Becker, Lars Benedikt Kaesberg, Andreas Stephan, Jan Philip Wahle, Terry Ruas, and Bela Gipp. 2025. cite115†Stay fo